                "1": {
                    "init": "comment t.smart_clamp_kf - a Kalman filter that caps each correction",
                    "name": "comment",
                    "id": 274,
                    "position_x": 24,
                    "position_y": -4,
                    "width": 746,
                    "height": 42,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "text",
                            "value": "t.smart_clamp_kf - a Kalman filter that caps each correction",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "font size",
                            "value": "48",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "comment": "t.smart_clamp_kf - a Kalman filter that caps each correction"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 1153,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1546,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Smooths streams of ordinary numbers with a Kalman filter that will not let any one frame pull it further than a set distance.\n\nUse it on positions or values from a tracker that occasionally throws a wild reading. A one-frame spike moves the output by no more than 'max jump', so it barely shows; steady change still comes through.\n\nHOW IT WORKS:\nA constant-velocity Kalman filter: it keeps a position and a velocity per value, predicts the next frame, and corrects towards the measurement. The correction is the INNOVATION - the measurement minus the prediction. Smart clamping caps the length of that innovation (across all of a stream's values together) at 'max jump' before it is used. A wild reading is treated as if it were only max jump away.\n\nThe cost is the other half of the bet: a real step bigger than max jump is also taken a bounded bite at a time, so the output walks to the new level over several frames instead of jumping. Set max jump just above the largest honest change between two frames.\n\nMANY STREAMS AT ONCE:\nThe input is a tensor of shape [streams, values]: one row per stream, all of them one moment in time. Every row gets its own filter state, so a whole body - 22 joints, one row each - is filtered in one operation. The first frame (and any change in the number of rows) builds the state afresh. A 1-D input of n values is one stream and comes back 1-D. A tensor with more dimensions is treated as rows of its last dimension and reshaped back.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.smart_clamp_kf\nNo arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.smart_clamp_kf\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe values. Each arrival runs one filter step and sends.\n\ndt (float, default 1/60):\nThe time between frames.\n\nresponsiveness (float, default 1.0):\nThe process noise: how freely the filter lets the velocity change. Higher follows changes sooner and smooths less.\n\nsmoothness (float, default 0.1):\nThe measurement noise: how much the filter distrusts each reading. Higher smooths more and lags more.\n\nmax jump (clamp) (float, default 0.1):\nThe largest innovation used in one frame, in the input's own units. 0 turns clamping off.\n\naccel_rejection (float, default 0, off):\nWhen the estimated acceleration reverses from one frame to the next, divide the process noise by (1 + 100 x this): much stiffer on chatter.\n\nmax_accel (deg/s^2):\nHas no effect on this node - only the quaternion filters use it.\n\nreset (button):\nThe next input becomes the state outright, at rest. The filter also starts this way, from its first input, so there is no glide in from zero when the patch opens.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfiltered:\nThe filtered values, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nMeasured on this input at the default 0.1: the spikes are almost gone (about 4 percent of each gets through), the jitter is six times smaller, and each step of 0.8 is reached in about a dozen frames. Drag max jump on the node: at 0.5 the steps take about four frames but a tenth of each spike shows; at 0.02 the output can no longer keep up even with the slow sine.\n\nTHE GLITCH OR THE MOVEMENT:\nA tracker throws two kinds of sudden change: a GLITCH, one bad frame that comes straight back, and a real QUICK MOVEMENT, a change that stays. On the frame it happens they look the same. Any filter that refuses the glitch will also hesitate on the quick movement; the filters in this family differ in how they make that bet. The demo input has both, so you can see each filter's choice.\n\nTHE FAMILY:\nt.ESEKF            ordinary numbers, three modes blended by motion\nt.quat_ESEKF       the same for quaternions (error-state)\nt.smart_clamp_kf   ordinary numbers, the correction per frame capped\nt.smart_clamp_quat_kf      the same for quaternions, plus an acceleration cap\nt.persistence_quat_kf      acceleration cap that gives way if the change persists\nt.hybrid_quat_kf   three-mode blending driving a smart-clamped filter\n\nRELATED:\nkalman_filter is a plain Kalman filter for single numbers; one_euro_filter adapts its smoothing to speed instead of clamping.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
